tinker_cookbook.distillation.train_on_policy:201 [[92mINFO[0m] <|im_system|>system<|im_middle|>You are Kimi, an AI assistant created by Moonshot AI.<|im_end|><|im_user|>user<|im_middle|>Explain why we spend the first year of a child's life teaching them to walk and talk and the rest telling them to shut up and sit down.<|im_end|><|im_assistant|>assistant<|im_middle|><think></think>It's one of life's most efficient ironies—we *engineer* the problem, then spend twelve years *managing* it.

The first year is basically a startup phase. You're the investor pouring everything into a tiny CEO who can't even hold their head up. The ROI is simple: verticality plus language. Walking means they're mobile enough to eventually fetch the remote. Talking means they can eventually explain *why* the remote is now in the toilet.

But here's where it gets complicated.

The very skills we celebrate—"*He said his first word!*"—become liabilities in institutional settings that require *selective* silence and *directed* stillness. We built a being capable of independent locomotion and unfiltered verbal expression, then discovered that civilization runs on sitting in rows and speaking in turn.

The real tragedy isn't the contradiction. It's the efficiency with which we strip away the wonder we just installed. The child who was *encouraged* to explore every surface with their mouth is now *discouraged* from exploring concepts with their voice.

We're not hypocrites, exactly. We're just running incompatible software versions—*Human.expansion_pack* followed immediately by *Human.institutional-compliance.patch*. The system works. It just works by breaking things in a very specific order.

And somehow, most of us turn out functional enough to do the same to the next generation.<|im_end|>
